WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:08.508
If you ever find yourself falling into a volcano,

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:10.810
there are a couple of
things you should know.

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:13.013
First off, when you reach the bottom,

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:16.449
the magma's not going to swallow 
you up as it does in the movies.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:21.788
In fact, the odds of your body even 
making it to the end of the fall are pretty low.

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:25.125
And considering what would happen 
if you did reach the bottom,

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:27.494
you'd probably be better off that way.

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:36.870
This is WHAT IF,

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.440
and here's what would 
happen if you fell into a volcano.

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:43.777
All it takes is one glimpse
of a volcano to tell you that it's

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:46.379
something you should probably avoid.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.848
The bright magma that spews from its core

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:51.851
can reach temperatures
of up to 1,000°C (1,832°F),

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:55.055
hot enough to completely 
incinerate a human body.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:58.725
And things aren't much better on the outside,

00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:04.931
as the rim of a volcano is filled with enough 
toxic gases to kill anyone who gets close to it.

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:07.100
So with all that considered,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.571
is it even possible to get close enough
to the mouth of a volcano to fall in?

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.544
There aren't many scenarios that could
lead to you tumbling inside a volcano,

00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:19.813
but that doesn't mean it's impossible.

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:22.715
For instance, let's say you're
out with a hiking group

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.684
approaching the peak of a volcano. 

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:28.421
As you get closer, you start 
to feel really hot and woozy;

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:31.391
you think you must just 
be getting exhausted, but no,

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:33.159
you're actually dying.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:38.465
You see, at the rim of a volcano 
temperatures will be hitting 500°C (932°F), 

00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:42.268
plus you’d be breathing in copious 
amounts of sulfur, methane,

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:44.637
carbon monoxide and ammonia.

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:47.841
It would only be a matter of 
time until asphyxiation sets in,

00:01:47.841 --> 00:01:51.177
which would cause you to  
lose consciousness and fall. 

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:54.214
But you probably wouldn't have 
made it close enough to the opening

00:01:54.214 --> 00:01:58.585
to actually fall into the volcano,  
so maybe this scenario doesn't work.

00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:02.489
If we want to understand what 
would happen if you fell into a volcano,

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:05.325
we're probably best to 
avoid the rim altogether

00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:07.861
and just drop you in from a helicopter.

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:11.164
Maybe you're an aerial photographer, 
just about to capture the

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:13.666
bubbling beauty of the lava lake below

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:17.403
when the helicopter starts to whip. 
back and forth uncontrollably.

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:21.441
Ash clouds that hang above volcanoes 
have been known to wreak havoc

00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:24.744
with airplane engines and 
flight components in general,

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:28.748
and in the ensuing chaos, 
you could get thrown from the chopper!

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:31.484
The fall itself isn't going to last long for you,

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:35.488
because within seconds you'll probably 
have passed out from lack of oxygen,

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:38.158
and ignited into flames from the radiant heat. 

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:40.493
And it doesn't get much better after that. 

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:45.098
Magma can be thousands to millions
of times more viscous than water,

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:49.669
which means when your body makes 
contact with it you are going to hit hard.

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:54.140
Like, hard enough to break most 
of the bones in your body.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:59.946
Ok so flaming skin, seared lungs, broken 
bones and now you’re stuck on the surface

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:04.551
since it is generally agreed lava and 
magma don’t let people sink,

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:06.686
at least not more than a few centimeters.

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:09.689
Oh, and did we mention that 
you'd ignite into another

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:11.758
ball of flames when you hit the surface? 

00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:16.462
In the blink of an eye, it would be just  
your bones and ashes on top of the lava,

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:18.598
and your bones won’t stick around long.

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:24.837
So if you fell into a volcano, it's likely that the 
only way you're getting out is in an urn.

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:28.908
But, surprisingly enough, there 
have been some survivors.

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:34.214
In 2007, a Maasai porter fell into
a volcano but managed to pull himself out;

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:38.484
despite suffering severe burns 
to one arm and both of his legs. 

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:41.087
The main reason for his being 
able to make it out alive

00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:44.958
is because the lava he fell 
into was natrocarbonatite lava,

00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:48.828
which is only half as hot as most other lavas. 

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:53.066
In general, though, it's a good idea 
to avoid volcanoes altogether.

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:58.771
Sure it would take a very specific set of 
circumstances for anyone to fall inside one, but

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:02.742
but there are plenty of other threats 
that could kill you before that happened.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:06.746
Maybe one day we'll be able to find a
purpose for a volcano's destructive nature,

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:09.482
like using it for a waste incinerator,

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:12.585
but that's a topic for another WHAT IF.

